From 2d99ef589d3a47245f3906c58492ac7831c80248 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 15:43:07 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/31] aout: re-establish the timing reference after a flush

aout_DecSynchronize() picks its thresholds from sync.discontinuity: when
it is set, any offset is absorbed at once by inserting silence or by
flushing, and the drift is reset to zero. When it is clear, offsets from
AOUT_MAX_PTS_ADVANCE up to three times AOUT_MAX_PTS_DELAY are made up by
resampling instead.

aout_DecFlush() cleared sync.end but left sync.discontinuity alone, and
the flag is only raised by a discontinuous block or a dropped buffer and
lowered after every block played. So a seek, or a pause whose output
cannot be paused, resumed with the flag clear and left the offset the
flush had just created to be "caught up" by resampling - detuning the
stream to chase a position it had already been asked to jump to.

Raise the flag in aout_DecFlush() so playback simply resumes from where
it was told to, as it already does on the 4.0 branch where the timing
reference is invalidated on reset.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/audio_output/dec.c | 4 ++++
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/audio_output/dec.c b/src/audio_output/dec.c
index 6e7ea17599..08802319d9 100644
--- a/src/audio_output/dec.c
+++ b/src/audio_output/dec.c
@@ -477,6 +477,10 @@ void aout_DecFlush (audio_output_t *aout, bool wait)
         /* Drop the correction with the data it was computed for. */
         aout_StopResampling (aout);
     }
+
+    /* The offset a flush leaves behind is not drift; do not resample to
+     * catch it up. */
+    owner->sync.discontinuity = true;
     aout_OutputUnlock (aout);
 }
 
